      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the last three digits of \\(3^{12000}\\), we need to determine \\(3^{12000} \\mod 1000\\). We are given that \\(3^{400} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\\), which means that the order of 3 modulo 1000 is 400. This means that \\(3^{400} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\\), and the powers of 3 repeat every 400 terms in the form of \\(3^{400k} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\\) for any integer \\(k\\).\n\nWe need to find \\(3^{12000} \\mod 1000\\). We can express 12000 as \\(12000 = 30 \\times 400\\), so:\n\\[\n3^{12000} = (3^{400})^{30} \\equiv 1^{30} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\n\\]\nThus, the last three digits of \\(3^{12000}\\) are \\(\\boxed{001}\\).",
